Ladybird lays out pinned tabs in expanded vertical tabs as a wrapping
grid of favicon tiles. Chromium’s PinnedTabContainerView does the same,
animating them on AnimationAxis::kHorizontalWrappingVertically. So does
the vertical pinned-tabs-container in Firefox, which lays them out with
grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(...)).

Once the pinned tabs need more than half the column, Ladybird scrolls
them with the unpinned tabs, as one list. Firefox and Chromium both give
pinned tabs a scrolling area of their own instead. In Firefox, the
#vertical-pinned-tabs-splitter resizes the #pinned-tabs-container, which
scrolls by itself. And Chromium’s TabStripView keeps them in its own
pinned_tabs_scroll_view_, apart from the unpinned_tabs_scroll_view_.

While collapsed vertical tabs peek open on hover, Ladybird keeps the
pinned tabs in rows, one tab each, with their titles. Firefox does the
same whenever its sidebar.visibility pref is expand-on-hover: Its
vertical #pinned-tabs-container then takes grid-template-columns: 1fr,
and keeps the tab labels showing.
